    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and verified. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `bulkSignupWarning` rule for `POST /api/owners`: when **more than 80** owners have already been created for the new owner's registration date, the response includes `bulkSignupWarning: true`; otherwise `false`. The value is computed at creation time and persisted on the owner (mirroring the existing `namesakeCount` / `membershipNumber` pattern), so it's returned by both the create response and later `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n**Files changed:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `bulkSignupWarning` boolean (`readOnly`) property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes the field.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `Boolean bulkSignupWarning` field (column `bulk_signup_warning`) with getter/setter. MapStruct auto-maps it into the DTO by name.\n\n3. **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 in `addOwner`, set `owner.setBulkSignupWarning(...)` before saving, plus a new `bulkSignupWarning(LocalDate)` helper that counts existing owners sharing that registration date and returns `count > 80` (the same accumulation path as the existing `dailyLimitReached` daily-cap rule).\n\n4. **DB schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres` `schema.sql`) \u2014 added the `bulk_signup_warning` column.\n\n**Tests:** `./mvnw test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. The `AcceptanceTest.coreNoBulkWarningUnderThreshold` case (a fresh create reads back `$.bulkSignupWarning == false`) passes, and I confirmed the generated `OwnerDto` contains the field.\n\nOne design note worth surfacing: I interpreted \"created today\" as the owner's registration date (consistent with the existing daily-limit rule, which the test comment explicitly says shares the same accumulation path), and used a strict `> 80` boundary per the spec's \"more than 80.\" Only the `false` branch is exercised by the provided test; the `> 80` branch is covered structurally via the shared counting logic.",
